# 3400-3000 BCE: monuments and axes vs elements of symbolic capital
Grid: 100-year bins, both endpoints inclusive -> N = 5 anchors (3400, 3300, 3200, 3100, 3000 BCE).
CE = -BCE, calBP = 1950 - BCE; time_series_data.csv is the authoritative time axis.
Z values are already standardised per variable over the full 37-anchor series.

## Hypothesis (answer)
Between 3400 and 3000 BCE the number of monuments (MonumentCount / ZMonument) and of axes & celts
(AxesCelts / ZBeil) do **not** covary positively with the elements of symbolic capital; they rise and fall
together as a single shared peak centred on the 3200-3100 BCE bins, while symbolic capital is either
static (Dagger constant at Z = -0.58; HatchetSword range 0.01 Z) or moving in the opposite direction
(House Size falls from its EN/MN maximum of Z = +2.05 at 3400 BCE to ~Z = 0). The best data-grounded
hypothesis is therefore: **in 3400-3000 BCE monument and axes/celts numbers are driven by depositional /
economic activity and by population dynamics rather than by symbolic capital - Monument Count and
Axes/Celts track each other (r = +0.87) and hoards (Depot, r = +0.85 / +0.93), and are unrelated to Belau
pollen openness levels (r = -0.02 / -0.22), whereas the only symbolic-capital indicator with any
in-window variance, House Size, shows a weak and sign-unstable negative level association with both
targets (r = -0.35 and -0.17, exact permutation p = 0.60 and 0.74) that reverses in the changes view for
Axes/Celts (r = +0.50). Symbolic capital matters, if at all, with a lag of ~200 years and outside the
window: in the 3800-2800 BCE context, House Size leads Monument Count (r = +0.68 at +2 bins) and
Axes/Celts (r = +0.56).**

